g>Christian Watson</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 ATL hold WR1s to just 52 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><p><strong>Drake London</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 GB hold WR1s to just 48 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Bijan Robinson</strong> is a bell-cow (65% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.50 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Christian Watson</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 ATL surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans GB.</strong> As a 8-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Josh Jacobs</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile ATL throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Drake London</strong>.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>GB \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>GB are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>36%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 8-point favorite projected to lead for 39% of snaps, <strong>GB</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Josh Jacobs</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>12 carries for 51 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as GB salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Christian Watson</strong> (6 targets, 56 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when GB lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tucker Kraft</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 52 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>ATL \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>ATL are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>33%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 ATL project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Bijan Robinson</strong>'s rushing projection (15 carries, 71 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Drake London</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, ATL funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>7 targets for 54 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Kyle Pitts</strong> (TE, 8 targets, 64 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 8-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the 